-- 8JP5 proof (id 102): link c_beta > 0 to the corollary, local finiteness to the lemma
UPDATE proofs SET body_tex = '\emph{A one-sided bound.} Put $g(u) = \mathcal G_\psi(e^u)$. Then $g$ is real, $|g(u)| \le C_\psi e^{2u}$, and
\[ \Phi_\psi(s) = \int_0^\infty g(u) e^{-su}\,du. \]
Fix $\sigma = 336$ and, for $k \ge 0$, set
\[ K_k(u) = \frac{u^k e^{-\sigma u}}{k!} \ge 0, \qquad A_k(s) = \frac{(-1)^k}{k!}\Phi_\psi^{(k)}(s), \qquad\text{so}\qquad A_k(\sigma + i\tau) = \int_0^\infty g(u) K_k(u) e^{-i\tau u}\,du. \]
Since $|g| + g = 2g_+$ with $g_+ = \max(g, 0)$,
\[ |A_k(\sigma + i\tau)| + \Re A_k(\sigma) \le 2\int_0^\infty g_+(u) K_k(u)\,du \qquad (\tau \in \R). \]
So only an \emph{upper} bound for $g$ is needed. Take $Y$ from the hypothesis, and
\[ U = \log Y, \qquad k = \lceil 420U \rceil. \]
The kernel $K_k$ peaks at $u = k/\sigma \approx \frac54 U$, inside $[U, \frac32 U]$, where $g_+(u) \le e^{\theta u}$. That range contributes at most
\[ \int_0^\infty e^{\theta u} K_k(u)\,du = (\sigma - \theta)^{-k-1}. \]
On the tails use $g_+ \le C_\psi e^{2u}$; a direct computation bounds them by $O_\psi(336^{-k})$. With $D_* = \sigma - \theta$ we get, for an unbounded sequence of $k$ and uniformly in $\tau$,
\[ |A_k(\sigma + i\tau)| + \Re A_k(\sigma) \le C_* D_*^{-k}. \tag{$*$} \]

\emph{Cauchy''s estimate.} If $\rho$ is the only pole of $\Phi_\psi$ in a closed disk $\overline B(z, r) \subset \left\{ s \in \C \;\middle|\; \Re s > 0 \right\}$, with residue $c$, then, with a constant independent of $k$,
\[ A_k(z) = \frac{c}{(z - \rho)^{k+1}} + O(r^{-k}). \]

\emph{Contradiction.} Suppose some $\rho_0 \in Z$ has $\Re \rho_0 > \theta$. Let
\[ D = \min\bigl(D_*, \operatorname{dist}(\sigma, Z)\bigr), \]
so the disk $B(\sigma, D)$ contains no pole.

(a) Some pole $\rho_1$ has $\sigma - \Re\rho_1 < D$. For $\tau$ near $\Im \rho_1$, a disk about $z = \sigma + i\tau$ of fixed radius less than $D$ contains $\rho_1$, and for $\tau$ in a short interval these disks meet only finitely many poles. Two poles with different imaginary parts are equidistant from $z$ for at most one $\tau$; two with equal imaginary parts never are, as both lie left of $\Re s = \sigma$. Avoiding these finitely many $\tau$, $z$ has a unique nearest pole $\rho$, at distance $d < D$. Choose $d < r < D$ with $\rho$ the only pole in $\overline B(z, r)$. Since $\overline B(\sigma, r)$ has no pole and the residue $c$ is nonzero by \hyperref[8JN4]{the lemma},
\[ |A_k(z)| + \Re A_k(\sigma) \ge |c|\, d^{-k-1} - O(r^{-k}). \]
Multiplying $(*)$ by $d^k$ and letting $k \to \infty$ gives $|c|/d \le 0$.

(b) Every pole has $\sigma - \Re\rho \ge D$. Applied to $\rho_0$ this gives $D \le \sigma - \Re\rho_0 < D_*$, so $D = \operatorname{dist}(\sigma, Z)$, attained (as $Z$ is \hyperref[8JS5]{locally finite}) at some $\rho$ with
\[ |\sigma - \rho| = D \le \sigma - \Re\rho. \]
Hence $\rho = \beta = \sigma - D$ is real and the unique nearest pole, with residue $c_\beta > 0$ by \hyperref[8JS7]{the corollary}. For some $D < r < D_*$,
\[ \Re A_k(\sigma) = c_\beta D^{-k-1} + O(r^{-k}), \]
while $(*)$ with $\tau = 0$ gives $2\Re A_k(\sigma) \le C_* D_*^{-k}$. Multiplying by $D^k$ gives $c_\beta / D \le 0$.

Both cases are impossible, so $\Re\rho \le \theta$ for all $\rho \in Z$. The positivity of the residue at real poles is what lets a \emph{one-sided} bound exclude real zeros.', body_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-24 10:45:00', updated_by = 'claude' WHERE id = 102;
INSERT INTO revisions (entity, entity_key, revision, operation_id, snapshot_json, author) VALUES ('proof', '102', 3, 'mellin-split-2026-11-24', '{"result_id":113,"body_tex":"\\emph{A one-sided bound.} Put $g(u) = \\mathcal G_\\psi(e^u)$. Then $g$ is real, $|g(u)| \\le C_\\psi e^{2u}$, and\n\\[ \\Phi_\\psi(s) = \\int_0^\\infty g(u) e^{-su}\\,du. \\]\nFix $\\sigma = 336$ and, for $k \\ge 0$, set\n\\[ K_k(u) = \\frac{u^k e^{-\\sigma u}}{k!} \\ge 0, \\qquad A_k(s) = \\frac{(-1)^k}{k!}\\Phi_\\psi^{(k)}(s), \\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad A_k(\\sigma + i\\tau) = \\int_0^\\infty g(u) K_k(u) e^{-i\\tau u}\\,du. \\]\nSince $|g| + g = 2g_+$ with $g_+ = \\max(g, 0)$,\n\\[ |A_k(\\sigma + i\\tau)| + \\Re A_k(\\sigma) \\le 2\\int_0^\\infty g_+(u) K_k(u)\\,du \\qquad (\\tau \\in \\R). \\]\nSo only an \\emph{upper} bound for $g$ is needed. Take $Y$ from the hypothesis, and\n\\[ U = \\log Y, \\qquad k = \\lceil 420U \\rceil. \\]\nThe kernel $K_k$ peaks at $u = k/\\sigma \\approx \\frac54 U$, inside $[U, \\frac32 U]$, where $g_+(u) \\le e^{\\theta u}$. That range contributes at most\n\\[ \\int_0^\\infty e^{\\theta u} K_k(u)\\,du = (\\sigma - \\theta)^{-k-1}. \\]\nOn the tails use $g_+ \\le C_\\psi e^{2u}$; a direct computation bounds them by $O_\\psi(336^{-k})$. With $D_* = \\sigma - \\theta$ we get, for an unbounded sequence of $k$ and uniformly in $\\tau$,\n\\[ |A_k(\\sigma + i\\tau)| + \\Re A_k(\\sigma) \\le C_* D_*^{-k}. \\tag{$*$} \\]\n\n\\emph{Cauchy''s estimate.} If $\\rho$ is the only pole of $\\Phi_\\psi$ in a closed disk $\\overline B(z, r) \\subset \\left\\{ s \\in \\C \\;\\middle|\\; \\Re s > 0 \\right\\}$, with residue $c$, then, with a constant independent of $k$,\n\\[ A_k(z) = \\frac{c}{(z - \\rho)^{k+1}} + O(r^{-k}). \\]\n\n\\emph{Contradiction.} Suppose some $\\rho_0 \\in Z$ has $\\Re \\rho_0 > \\theta$. Let\n\\[ D = \\min\\bigl(D_*, \\operatorname{dist}(\\sigma, Z)\\bigr), \\]\nso the disk $B(\\sigma, D)$ contains no pole.\n\n(a) Some pole $\\rho_1$ has $\\sigma - \\Re\\rho_1 < D$. For $\\tau$ near $\\Im \\rho_1$, a disk about $z = \\sigma + i\\tau$ of fixed radius less than $D$ contains $\\rho_1$, and for $\\tau$ in a short interval these disks meet only finitely many poles. Two poles with different imaginary parts are equidistant from $z$ for at most one $\\tau$; two with equal imaginary parts never are, as both lie left of $\\Re s = \\sigma$. Avoiding these finitely many $\\tau$, $z$ has a unique nearest pole $\\rho$, at distance $d < D$. Choose $d < r < D$ with $\\rho$ the only pole in $\\overline B(z, r)$. Since $\\overline B(\\sigma, r)$ has no pole and the residue $c$ is nonzero by \\hyperref[8JN4]{the lemma},\n\\[ |A_k(z)| + \\Re A_k(\\sigma) \\ge |c|\\, d^{-k-1} - O(r^{-k}). \\]\nMultiplying $(*)$ by $d^k$ and letting $k \\to \\infty$ gives $|c|/d \\le 0$.\n\n(b) Every pole has $\\sigma - \\Re\\rho \\ge D$. Applied to $\\rho_0$ this gives $D \\le \\sigma - \\Re\\rho_0 < D_*$, so $D = \\operatorname{dist}(\\sigma, Z)$, attained (as $Z$ is \\hyperref[8JS5]{locally finite}) at some $\\rho$ with\n\\[ |\\sigma - \\rho| = D \\le \\sigma - \\Re\\rho. \\]\nHence $\\rho = \\beta = \\sigma - D$ is real and the unique nearest pole, with residue $c_\\beta > 0$ by \\hyperref[8JS7]{the corollary}. For some $D < r < D_*$,\n\\[ \\Re A_k(\\sigma) = c_\\beta D^{-k-1} + O(r^{-k}), \\]\nwhile $(*)$ with $\\tau = 0$ gives $2\\Re A_k(\\sigma) \\le C_* D_*^{-k}$. Multiplying by $D^k$ gives $c_\\beta / D \\le 0$.\n\nBoth cases are impossible, so $\\Re\\rho \\le \\theta$ for all $\\rho \\in Z$. The positivity of the residue at real poles is what lets a \\emph{one-sided} bound exclude real zeros.","status":"ai_generated","generated_by":"claude","generator_detail":"claude-opus-5-5","reviewed_by":null,"review_notes":null,"position":1}', 'claude');
